  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 138 PM EDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nDog days of summer continue through at least Saturday with the stout \nridge staying relatively locked in place over the southern United \nStates. Heat Advisory that was in effect for today has been extended \nto now include Saturday. Temperatures and Heat Index values will be \nrelatively similar both today and tomorrow, with triple digits \nvalues expected for much of the eastern Tennessee Valley. If you \nplan on attending any outdoor activities this weekend it's extremely \nimportant to plan for these high temperatures. Drink plenty of water \nand stay in the shade as much as possible.\n\nIn addition, the hot temperatures today and tomorrow will bring a \ncouple of rounds of afternoon showers and thunderstorms. With the \nvery high temperatures some of the strongest storms could become \nsevere with damaging winds as the primary hazard.  Showers and \nstorms will be isolated in the Tennessee Valley today and more \nscattered in the higher elevations. \n\nOn Saturday, the trough dips into the Ohio Valley as the ridge moves \na bit west to be more over the Plains. At the surface, a weak cold \nfront will move through the region, bringing better chances for \nshowers and storms, especially north of Interstate 40. With better \nsynoptic lift in place on Saturday, there is also a higher chance \nfor strong to severe storms especially in extreme northeast \nTennessee and southwest Virginia, closer to the trough. With \nmoderate instability, weak deep layer shear and high freezing \nlevels, the primary threat will be damaging straight-line winds once \nagain.\n\nStarting Sunday we'll get a \"break\" from these near record \ntemperatures, but it's still going to be above normal for the \nforeseeable future as the stubborn ridge remains locked in place \nover the United States. There's a sliver of hope that heading into \nnext weekend we get a bit cooler as a couple of system moving across \nCanada try and break down the ridge.\n\nDaily Record High Maximum Temperatures\n\nDate    Chattanooga    Knoxville      Tri-Cities     Oak Ridge             \n09-04   102(1954)      101(1954)      99(1954)       100(1954)      \n09-05 104(1925) 103(1954) 99(1954) 102(1954)",
